---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<!-- about-v2 -->
<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="About Eric Nolan: technical product management, hands-on AI development, engineering, Scouting leadership, and practical building."
>
  <header class="subhero subhero--about">
    <div class="shell subhero-grid">
      <div class="subhero-copy">
        <div class="subhero-badges">
          <span>20+ YEARS TECH</span>
          <span>AI BUILDER</span>
          <span>PRODUCT LEADER</span>
          <span>EAGLE SCOUT</span>
        </div>
        <p class="subhero-kicker">ERIC NOLAN // BUILDER FIRST</p>
        <h1>Product judgment with dirt under its fingernails.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          I came up through software development, performance engineering, release delivery, and product leadership. Today I spend a lot of that range building and governing AI systems—close enough to the code to know when the architecture story is lying.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button button-pop" href="/work/">See the evidence →</a>
          <a class="button secondary case-secondary" href="/contact/">Start a conversation</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <figure class="builder-portrait">
        <img src="/eric-nolan.jpg" alt="Eric Nolan" width="800" height="800" fetchpriority="high" />
        <figcaption><strong>Eric Nolan</strong><span>Technical product leader. Hands-on builder.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section about-identity" aria-label="Career and identity snapshot">
    <div class="shell">
      <aside class="about-stack about-stack--horizontal" aria-label="Career and identity snapshot">
        <div class="about-stack__card about-stack__card--ai">
          <span>NOW</span>
          <strong>Technical Product + AI Development</strong>
          <small>Strategy, architecture, Python, orchestration, governance, integration, testing, rollout.</small>
        </div>
        <div class="about-stack__card about-stack__card--engineering">
          <span>BEFORE</span>
          <strong>Engineering + Release Systems</strong>
          <small>Software development, performance engineering, architecture, release and enterprise delivery.</small>
        </div>
        <div class="about-stack__card about-stack__card--scouting">
          <span>ALWAYS</span>
          <strong>Scouting + Service</strong>
          <small>Eagle Scout · Sachem · Cubmaster · Wood Badge.</small>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section about-career">
    <div class="shell career-grid">
      <div class="career-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow">Career arc</p>
        <h2>I did not arrive at AI from a slide deck.</h2>
        <p>
          More than two decades in technology gave me a useful bias: systems have to operate after the demo, people have to understand them, and someone eventually has to debug the consequences.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="career-timeline" aria-label="Career progression">
        <article>
          <span>1998 → 2004</span>
          <strong>Application Development</strong>
          <p>Enterprise software, coding, testing, packaging, support, troubleshooting, and documentation.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>2004 → 2013</span>
          <strong>Release + Performance Engineering</strong>
          <p>Release systems, capacity, performance analysis, launch readiness, architecture, vendors, and production realities.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>2013 → 2020</span>
          <strong>Product + Business Translation</strong>
          <p>Epics, requirements, prioritization, stakeholder alignment, roadmaps, risks, dependencies, and delivery.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>2020 → NOW</span>
          <strong>Technical Product + AI Systems</strong>
          <p>AI product strategy, hands-on development, orchestration, APIs, governance, testing, developer enablement, and rollout.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--dark about-values">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading section-heading--punch">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">What I optimize for</p>
          <h2>Useful. Explainable. Durable.</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="section-intro">
          I like ambitious technology. I just don't confuse ambition with complexity.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="principle-grid principle-grid--punch">
        <article class="principle">
          <span>01 // EVIDENCE</span>
          <h3>Show the work.</h3>
          <p>Public implementations and case studies are more useful than stacking adjectives into a headline.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="principle">
          <span>02 // BOUNDARIES</span>
          <h3>Know what the system may do.</h3>
          <p>Authority, privacy, approvals, and failure behavior should be designed—not discovered in production.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="principle">
          <span>03 // JUDGMENT</span>
          <h3>Do not automate the wrong thing faster.</h3>
          <p>Product sense still matters. Sometimes the best AI architecture is less AI.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--scouting">
    <div class="shell scouting-grid">
      <div class="scouting-emblem">
        <div class="scouting-mark egg-mark egg-mark--scouting" data-egg="UNYIELDING CLAW // SACHEM" data-trail-hover="about-uc" tabindex="0" role="note" aria-label="Sawblade with a fleur-de-lis at its center. Hidden Trail detail.">
          <img src="/brand-mark.svg?v=3" width="176" height="176" alt="" aria-hidden="true" />
          <svg class="scouting-mark__fleur" viewBox="0 0 64 64" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <circle cx="32" cy="32" r="31" fill="#0b1f33" stroke="#6e9fe6" stroke-width="1.5" />
            <g fill="#dceaff" stroke="#91b8f0" stroke-width=".8" stroke-linejoin="round">
              <path d="M32 9C25 18 25 27 32 35c7-8 7-17 0-26Z" />
              <path d="M28 39c-3-10-8-15-13-14-6 1-8 7-5 12 2 4 7 6 11 4-4-2-5-5-3-8 3-3 7 0 8 6Z" />
              <path d="M36 39c3-10 8-15 13-14 6 1 8 7 5 12-2 4-7 6-11 4 4-2 5-5 3-8-3-3-7 0-8 6Z" />
              <path d="M26 38h12l-2 15-4 4-4-4-2-15Z" />
            </g>
            <path d="M23 41h18M26 47h12" fill="none" stroke="#91b8f0" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round" />
          </svg>
        </div>
        <span class="scouting-emblem__caption">UNYIELDING CLAW // THE ORIGIN</span>
        <button class="trail-marker trail-marker--scouting" type="button" data-trail-marker="about-origin" aria-label="Hidden trail marker: Origin Story">◇</button>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Scouting is not a résumé footnote</p>
        <h2>Unyielding Claw has a history.</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Unyielding Claw is my Mic-O-Say name.</strong> I am an Eagle Scout and a Sachem in the Heart of America Council's Tribe of Mic-O-Say. Scouting has been a major thread in my life through leadership, service, mentoring, Cub Scouting, Wood Badge, and helping people grow into responsibility.
        </p>
        <p>
          The name became my public engineering identity because it already meant something to me long before GitHub did.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://www.hoac-bsa.org/micosay">Learn about Mic-O-Say from the Heart of America Council →</a>
      </div>
      <aside class="scouting-callout">
        <span>LEADERSHIP THREAD</span>
        <strong>Eagle Scout</strong>
        <strong>Sachem, Tribe of Mic-O-Say</strong>
        <strong>Cubmaster</strong>
        <strong>Wood Badge</strong>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section about-personal">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading section-heading--punch">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Off the clock, same brain</p>
          <h2>The hobbies look unrelated. The pattern isn't.</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="section-intro">
          I like learning systems, adapting plans instead of worshiping them, and making something tangible. Sometimes that is code. Sometimes it is a jig, a photo, a brisket, a bowling adjustment, or a crochet hook.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="personal-mosaic">
        <article class="personal-tile personal-tile--photo">
          <span>PHOTOGRAPHY</span>
          <strong>Capture the moment.</strong>
          <small>Landscapes, abstracts, sports, and seeing the frame before overthinking the settings.</small>
        </article>
        <article class="personal-tile personal-tile--wood">
          <span>WOODWORKING</span>
          <strong>Adapt the plan.</strong>
          <small>Build for the actual use, the tools on hand, and the space that exists.</small>
        </article>
        <article class="personal-tile personal-tile--bbq">
          <span>BBQ + WHISKEY</span>
          <strong>Turn hobbies into systems.</strong>
          <small>Telemetry became PitLogic. Club history became Tipsy Bunghole. Apparently I cannot leave a workflow alone.</small>
        </article>
        <article class="personal-tile personal-tile--bowling">
          <span>BOWLING</span>
          <strong>Measure what changes.</strong>
          <small>Equipment, patterns, sessions, adjustments, and enough data to separate memory from evidence.</small>
        </article>
        <article class="personal-tile personal-tile--crochet">
          <span class="egg-inline" data-egg="Not a bug. Also not a scarf yet." data-trail-hover="about-crochet" tabindex="0" role="note" aria-label="Yes, crochet. Hidden note: not a bug. Also not a scarf yet.">YES, CROCHET</span>
          <strong>Hands busy. Brain working.</strong>
          <small>Not every useful build needs a compiler or a table saw.</small>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section about-builder">
    <div class="shell bridge-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Builder is the through-line</p>
        <h2>Software. Systems. Jigs. Teams.</h2>
        <p class="section-intro">
          The medium changes, but I keep coming back to the same thing: understand what has to work, build enough structure to make it dependable, and improve it with evidence from actual use.
        </p>
      </div>
      <a class="bridge-card" href="/woodworking/">
        <span>AFTER HOURS</span>
        <strong>Nolan's Workshop</strong>
        <small>Same problem-solving habit. More sawdust. →</small>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
